+## Duplicate alerts
+
+**Several identical pages in a row are usually one alert, not several.**
+Acknowledge once and work it as a single incident.
+
+One call to `send()` makes up to 3 attempts. It retries on a timeout (5s) or a
+5xx, and neither of those means the SMS went undelivered -- Kestrel may have
+sent it and lost the reply on the way back. Every attempt of one `send()`
+carries the same `Idempotency-Key`, which is what lets Kestrel collapse the
+retries into one delivery. Copies still reaching a phone means that key is not
+being honoured; that is a Kestrel-side question, not a caller bug.
+
+What you see tells you which it is:
+
+| On your phone | What happened | What to do |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| 2-3 identical messages inside ~15s | One alert, retried. | Ack once. |
+| More than 3 identical messages | Not the retry path -- 3 attempts is the hard ceiling. Something called `send()` more than once. | Ack once, then chase the caller. |
+| Identical messages minutes apart | Same: a caller re-alerting, or two alert sources covering one condition. | Ack once, then chase the caller. |
+
+The ~15s comes from the retry schedule: three attempts, each up to a 5s
+timeout, 2s apart. A fast-failing Kestrel compresses all copies into ~5s.
+
+The count never tells you anything about severity. Three copies is not a worse
+incident than one; it only means the first attempts were not acknowledged by
+Kestrel.
+
 ## Failures
 
-A send that fails raises `RuntimeError` once the client has stopped retrying.
-Treat the alert as unsent and page the secondary by hand.
+`send()` raises, and the alert is unsent, in two different ways:
+
+- `RuntimeError` -- all 3 attempts failed on timeouts or 5xx. The message text
+  names the last failure.
+- `requests.HTTPError` -- Kestrel rejected the request outright (4xx: bad
+  number, bad token, rate limit). No retry happens; see Rate limits.
+
+Either way, treat the alert as unsent and page the secondary by hand. Code that
+catches only `RuntimeError` will miss the 4xx case.
 
 ## Rate limits
 
 Kestrel accepts 30 messages a minute per account. Above that it answers 429 and
-the client raises straight away without retrying.
+the client raises `requests.HTTPError` straight away without retrying.
